    <briefdescription>
<para>Functions used to compose logical expressions in tests. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The functions <computeroutput>mt::all_of()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>mt::negate()</computeroutput> combine multiple conditions, allowing expressive and concise test logic. <computeroutput>mt::all_of()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput> take two or more operands, and <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput> is an inclusive or: it is true also when all the conditions are true. A chain of the same operation is reported as a single group, for example <computeroutput>(a and b and c)</computeroutput>.</para>
<para>All the operands are evaluated before the check, so the functions do not stop at the first operand that decides the result, like the standard algorithms of the same names. After a <computeroutput>using namespace std</computeroutput>, an unqualified call with three or more operands of the same type selects the standard algorithm; call the functions as <computeroutput>mt::all_of()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput>.</para>
<para>The functions have descriptive names, since <computeroutput>and</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>or</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>not</computeroutput> are alternative tokens and cannot be used as identifiers. Using these logical functions allows developers to express compound conditions clearly and improve readability.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Example</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>compound<sp/>conditions&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::all_of<sp/>(mt::eq<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>42),</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>mt::eq<sp/>(compute_float<sp/>(),<sp/>42.0),</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>mt::lt<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>43)));</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">});</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para>The overloaded logical operators of the <computeroutput>micro_test_plus::operators</computeroutput> namespace are deprecated since 5.1.0, in favour of these functions.  </para>
    </detaileddescription>
